# Every variable compose.yaml reads. Copy this file to .env beside it, which git ignores, and
# fill it in on the machine that runs the hosted copy; tests/agree.test.sh checks nothing the
# compose file reads is missing here. Never copy a value from another environment, the dev
# stack's infra/.env included.
#
# Each password goes into a database URL as it is, so it is letters and digits only. Make each
# one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
# The ten are read when the Postgres volume is first created, by the image's entrypoint and by
# the scripts in infra/postgres/initdb/; changing one afterwards changes nothing in the database.

# Superuser, for maintenance by hand. No service connects as it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The core's database: the owner runs core-migrate, the app role is what the running core
# connects as.
CORE_OWNER_PASSWORD=
CORE_APP_PASSWORD=

# The brain's database, the same two roles, for brain-migrate and the running brain.
BRAIN_OWNER_PASSWORD=
BRAIN_APP_PASSWORD=

# Review's database, the same two roles, for review-migrate and the running review.
REVIEW_OWNER_PASSWORD=
REVIEW_APP_PASSWORD=

# Dev flow's database, the same two roles, for devflow-migrate and the running Dev flow.
DEVFLOW_OWNER_PASSWORD=
DEVFLOW_APP_PASSWORD=

# Dev flow's code host, github here. Everywhere else, the laptop's tests, CI and the runner's
# stack, Dev flow fakes it, in memory, with a banner on every page; that must never happen where
# real data is, so the hosted copy names the mode, and a release refuses an env file without it.
# See "The code host" in devflow/README.md.
DEVFLOW_CODE_HOST=github

# The key Dev flow's vault seals each user's secrets with, such as their GitHub tokens, so a copy
# of the database, a backup included, does not reveal them. An id and 32 random bytes, made with:
#   printf 'k1:%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
# Keep it in the password manager: it is not in the backups, and without it nothing sealed opens,
# so each secret is entered again, a GitHub link by linking again. See "The vault" in
# devflow/README.md, which also says how a key is changed.
DEVFLOW_VAULT_KEY=

# Keys rows may still be sealed with while a new one replaces them, comma separated in the same
# form, each listed until every secret has been put again under the current key.
# DEVFLOW_VAULT_OLD_KEYS=

# Dev flow's GitHub App, which it reads repositories as, with keys that can only read. Each value
# is on the app's page on GitHub: Settings, Developer settings, GitHub Apps, then Edit beside the
# app. See "Settings" in devflow/README.md.
#
# The client id, under About on the General tab, "Client ID", Iv23li followed by the rest.
DEVFLOW_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# A client secret, under "Client secrets" on the General tab, "Generate a new client secret". GitHub
# shows it once; keep it in the password manager.
DEVFLOW_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The path on this machine of the app's private key, under "Private keys" on the General tab,
# "Generate a private key", which downloads a .pem file. Keep it outside the repository, chmod 600,
# and in the password manager. compose.yaml mounts it read only into Dev flow's container, whose
# user, uid 1654, must read it; Docker Desktop on macOS shows a mounted file to the container as its
# own, so 600 is enough there, while on Linux the file must be readable by uid 1654.
DEVFLOW_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
# The app's slug, the last part of its public address, github.com/apps/<slug>, shown as "Public
# link" on the General tab.
DEVFLOW_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=

# platform_backup, the role the backup job dumps every database as, made by
# postgres/backup-role.sh.
BACKUP_DB_PASSWORD=

# The key every token is signed with, made for this copy alone, once the passwords and the
# origin above are set and the images are built, from infra/hosted/ with the copy's project name
# in place of <project>:
#   docker compose -p <project> run --rm --no-deps -T core keygen >> .env
# which appends this line, so it stays commented out here. See "The signing key" in
# Core/docs/core-architecture.md.
# CORE_SIGNING_KEY=

# The platform's origin exactly as a browser sends it, scheme and host and any port, such as
# https://platform.example.ts.net. Every service checks the Origin header against it.
PLATFORM_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

# Where the proxy is published, plain HTTP, which is terminated in front of it. Unset is
# 127.0.0.1:9080, the loopback only.
# HOSTED_HTTP_BIND=127.0.0.1:9080

# The tag of every image, platform-hosted/<service>:<tag>. Unset is dev; a release tags by commit.
# PLATFORM_RELEASE=dev

# --- Backups, see "Backups" in README.md. The job checks each, and stops naming one that is
# missing or malformed. There is no mode without a bucket.

# The age public key every dump is encrypted to, age1 followed by the rest. The private key is
# made on another machine and is never on this one, see README.md.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=

# The bucket: its endpoint as an origin, such as https://s3.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com, its region,
# its name, and a key restricted to it without the right to delete.
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_REGION=
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
BACKUP_S3_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_KEY=

# The folder in the bucket each file goes under. Unset is platform.
# BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=platform

# The time of the nightly backup, HH:MM, in TZ. Unset is 03:00 in Europe/Stockholm. A TZ exported
# in the shell running compose wins over this file, as every variable does.
# BACKUP_AT=03:00
# TZ=Europe/Stockholm

# How many days a dump stays in the local volume after a successful backup. Unset is 7.
# BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL_DAYS=7
